    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A nomadic confederation joined herding tribes under one ruling clan and khan, and organised the whole people as an army in units of ten, a hundred, a thousand and ten thousand.</li><li>Steppe rulers lived largely on what they drew from settled empires by raids, tribute treaties and trade, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> did from Han <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>.</li><li>Disputed successions repeatedly split these empires, and by 1757 the guns, forts and frontier treaties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> had left the steppe no room for another.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>According to the Book of Han, Han envoys could enter the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> ruler’s tents only after setting aside their tallies of office and having their faces tattooed; the envoy Wang Wu agreed and was received with honour.</p></aside>
    </section>

