    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">209 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modu-chanyu">Modu Chanyu</a> takes power and unites the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>, founding the first great empire of the eastern steppe</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">198 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">After Emperor Gaozu’s defeat at Baideng, the Han buy peace with a princess and regular gifts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and grain</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">51 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Amid a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-civil-war">Xiongnu civil war</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huhanye">Huhanye</a> pays homage in person at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang’an</a> and accepts Han overlordship</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">552</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bumin-qaghan">Bumin Qaghan</a> overthrows the Rouran and founds the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-turkic-khaganate">First Turkic Khaganate</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1206</span><span class="rf-text">A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurultai">kurultai</a> at the source of the Onon river proclaims <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Temüjin</a> Genghis Khan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1260</span><span class="rf-text">Rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurultai">kurultais</a> elect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariq-boke">Ariq Böke</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-khan">Great Khan</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> begins to break apart</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1757</span><span class="rf-text">Qing armies crush the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungar</a> resistance, ending the last great nomadic empire of the steppe</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="works">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Works</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kurultai"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kurultai.jpg" alt="Kurultai" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kurultai">Kurultai</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Folio_248._Entertainment_given_by_Timur,_on_his_arrival_in_Samarkand._Zafarnama,_1533_(British_Library,_I.O._Islamic_137).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">British Library, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>A nomadic confederation united the herding peoples of the Eurasian steppe under one ruling clan and its supreme ruler, called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanyu">chanyu</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagan</a> or khan by the Turks and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>. Beneath him stood princes of the blood, the chiefs of the member tribes and commanders he appointed. Chinese sources describe the Xiongnu realm as divided between a left, or eastern, wing under the Wise King of the Left, usually the heir, and a right, or western, wing under the Wise King of the Right, each with paired lesser kings and governors, and below them commanders of a thousand, a hundred and ten men, so that the whole people was organised as an army. The Turkic khaganates were likewise divided between an eastern and a western ruler, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> reorganised the Mongols into units of ten, a hundred, a thousand and ten thousand under commanders chosen for loyalty and ability, deliberately mixing the old tribes, with an imperial guard of 10,000 at the centre.</p><p>Great decisions were taken in assembly. The Mongol <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurultai">kurultai</a>, a gathering of the ruling family, the imperial sons-in-law and the army commanders, elected each <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-khan">Great Khan</a>, planned campaigns and assigned commands, and a kurultai held without the senior princes from across the empire was regarded as illegitimate; regional kurultais chose the khans of states such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>. The Xiongnu chanyu met his chiefs each year at Longcheng, the “Dragon City”, and a prince who stayed away signalled revolt. Law combined custom with the ruler’s decrees: the Mongols’ <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yasa">Yasa</a> was attributed to Genghis Khan, though historians debate whether it was ever a written code.</p><p>The confederation depended on its settled neighbours. Pastoral nomads needed grain, cloth and metal they could not produce, and their rulers needed goods to reward followers. The anthropologist Thomas Barfield has called such polities “imperial confederacies”, autocratic and state-like towards the outside world but consultative and federal within, held together by the wealth their rulers drew from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> by raiding, tribute and trade. They differed from settled empires in having little bureaucracy and few cities and in drawing more from outside than from their own herds; from looser tribal alliances in having a permanent ruling house and a single army; and from conquest dynasties such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Khitan Liao</a> or the Mongol Yuan, which ruled farming lands through settled administrations.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sima-qian.jpg" alt="Sima Qian" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Si_maqian.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mounted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pastoralism">pastoralism</a> spread across the Eurasian steppe in the first millennium BCE, and with it the first nomadic powers known to writers in settled lands. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> described the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a> north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> in the 5th century BCE, with a royal tribe that regarded the other Scythians as its slaves, and told how <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> marched against them about 513 BCE. The first steppe empire in the full sense arose on the Mongolian plateau, soon after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin dynasty</a> united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 221 BCE. In 215 BCE the Qin general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meng-tian">Meng Tian</a> drove the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> ruler Touman from the Ordos Loop of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a>, and in 209 BCE Touman’s son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modu-chanyu">Modu Chanyu</a> took power. The Han historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a>, writing about a century later, tells how Modu trained his horsemen to shoot wherever his whistling arrow flew, put to death those who hesitated, and then aimed at his own father.</p><p>As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanyu">chanyu</a>, Modu turned the Xiongnu into a confederation able to raise and coordinate far larger armies. He crushed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donghu">Donghu</a> to the east, conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dingling">Dingling</a> of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> and drove the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu-corridor">Gansu corridor</a>, so that by his death in 174 BCE the Xiongnu dominated the steppe from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> to the oases of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a>. In 200 BCE he trapped Emperor Gaozu of Han, the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>, at Baideng, and two years later the Han bought peace. The Xiongnu set the pattern that later steppe powers followed: a ruling clan, a decimal army and wealth drawn from China.</p><p>Writers in settled lands analysed these powers long before modern theory. Sima Qian set out the Xiongnu ranks and customs and drew the sharp line between the settled Chinese and the nomads that later Chinese histories kept. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khaldun">Ibn Khaldun</a> argued in his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muqaddimah">Muqaddimah</a> of 1377 that peoples of the desert and steppe, bound by strong group feeling, asabiyya, conquer settled realms and then lose that cohesion within a few generations. Modern scholars disagree about what kind of polity a steppe empire was. Anatoly Khazanov stressed that pastoral nomads could never be self-sufficient; Barfield that their empires rose and fell in step with China’s; Nicola Di Cosmo that the Xiongnu state was born of crisis and the militarisation of steppe society; Nikolay Kradin that such empires were “supercomplex chiefdoms” rather than states; and David Sneath that they were aristocratic orders rather than tribes held together by kinship.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="history">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Through History</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han.jpg" alt="Emperor Wu of Han" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu of Han</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%BC%A2%E6%AD%A6%E5%B8%9D.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>A treaty of 198 BCE gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanyu">chanyu</a> a Han princess, regular gifts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and grain and recognition as an equal, and was renewed nine times with ever larger gifts until 135 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu of Han</a> broke with it in 133 BCE, and campaigns under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wei-qing">Wei Qing</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huo-qubing">Huo Qubing</a> drove the chanyu’s court north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobi-desert">Gobi</a> in 119 BCE. In a war between rival chanyus that began in 60 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huhanye">Huhanye</a> accepted Han overlordship and paid homage in person at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang’an</a> in 51 BCE; in 48 CE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> split for good into southern and northern realms. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> and then the Rouran, who made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagan</a> the title of their supreme ruler, held the eastern steppe in turn, while in the west the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> built an empire that under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a>, sole ruler from about 445 to 453, drew a tribute of 2,100 pounds of gold a year from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>.</p><p>In 552 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bumin-qaghan">Bumin Qaghan</a> overthrew the Rouran and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-turkic-khaganate">First Turkic Khaganate</a>, which within a generation stretched from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>. It split around 600 and fell to the Tang; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-turkic-khaganate">Second Turkic Khaganate</a> (682–744) left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkhon-inscriptions">Orkhon inscriptions</a>, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilge-qaghan">Bilge Qaghan</a> warned his people against the sweet words and soft silks of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> revived the form on a new scale. Proclaimed at a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurultai">kurultai</a> at the source of the Onon river in 1206, he broke the old tribal order and led his armies into northern China and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, and under his successors the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> became the largest contiguous land empire in history, stretching from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a> to eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><p>The deaths of Ögedei in 1241 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongke">Möngke</a> in 1259 called princes home to choose a successor, and in 1260 rival kurultais elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a> and his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariq-boke">Ariq Böke</a>. The empire broke into four realms: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a> in China, which fell in 1368, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Khanate</a> in Central Asia and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, whose successors, among them the Crimean and Kazakh khanates, lasted into the 18th century. The last great steppe empire was the Dzungar Khanate of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oirats">Oirat</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>, founded in the 1630s. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galdan-boshugtu-khan">Galdan Boshugtu Khan</a> fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kangxi-emperor">Kangxi Emperor</a> of the Qing until his defeat at Jao Modo in 1696, and after a succession crisis Qing armies conquered the khanate in 1755 and crushed the rising of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amursana">Amursana</a> by 1757. Massacres ordered under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qianlong-emperor">Qianlong Emperor</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> and flight destroyed most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungar</a> people.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="strengths">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Strengths</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sorghaghtani-beki"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sorghaghtani-beki.jpg" alt="Sorghaghtani Beki" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sorghaghtani-beki">Sorghaghtani Beki</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TuluiWithQueenSorgaqtani.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rashid al-Din, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Military historians have long stressed the effectiveness of the steppe armies. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mounted-archer">Mounted archers</a> raised in the saddle, each with several horses, could cover great distances fast, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/decimal-military-organisation">decimal organisation</a> let commanders move tens of thousands of men in coordinated columns. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> promoted men for loyalty and ability rather than birth: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebe">Jebe</a>, who according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secret-history-of-the-mongols">Secret History of the Mongols</a> had once shot the khan’s horse from under him in battle, became one of his leading generals. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> also learned quickly, taking Chinese and Muslim engineers into their armies for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-warfare">siege warfare</a>.</p><p>Historians also credit the confederations with joining distant worlds. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pax-mongolica">Pax Mongolica</a> of the 13th and 14th centuries goods, envoys and ideas crossed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurasia">Eurasia</a> more easily than ever before, helped by a network of relay stations, the yam, that carried officials and messages across the empire. Mongol rulers exempted the clergy of many religions from taxation, and in 1254 the Franciscan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-of-rubruck">William of Rubruck</a> took part in a debate between Christians, Muslims and Buddhists at the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongke">Möngke</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakorum">Karakorum</a>. Steppe rulers readily employed settled advisers: according to Chinese accounts, the Khitan statesman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yelu-chucai">Yelü Chucai</a> persuaded Ögedei that taxing the farmers of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> would yield more than turning their fields into pasture.</p><p>Barfield and others see in the confederation a shrewd answer to the problem of living beside a far richer neighbour. By raiding, threatening and negotiating, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> extracted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, grain and trading rights from the Han without trying to rule China, and so kept their own way of life intact. Power at the centre was shared: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurultai">kurultai</a> gave leading princes and commanders a say in choosing rulers and making war, and women of the ruling house could exercise great influence, as Töregene Khatun did as regent after Ögedei’s death and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorghaghtani-beki">Sorghaghtani Beki</a> did in winning the throne for her son Möngke in 1251. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khaldun">Ibn Khaldun</a> saw in the solidarity of such peoples the force that renewed the settled world.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="weaknesses">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Weaknesses</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hulagu-khan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hulagu-khan.jpg" alt="Hulagu Khan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hulagu-khan">Hulagu Khan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hulagu_Khan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rashid-al-Din Hamadani, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The confederations’ great weakness was succession. Barfield notes that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> custom of passing power to a dead ruler’s brother when his son was too young kept an adult on the throne but multiplied claimants in the next generation, and the first such crisis brought five rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanyu">chanyus</a> to arms in the 50s BCE. The historian Joseph Fletcher called the Turco-Mongol practice “bloody tanistry”: any male of the ruling house might claim the throne, and the ablest won by war or murder. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-turkic-khaganate">First Turkic Khaganate</a> split in two, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a>’s sons quarrelled over his subjects and were defeated by the Gepid king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardaric">Ardaric</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nedao">Battle of Nedao</a> in 454, and Kublai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariq-boke">Ariq Böke</a> fought for four years after 1260.</p><p>Because the ruler’s power rested on the wealth he could hand out, a confederation was only as strong as its flow of plunder and tribute. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a> stopped paying and went to war, the Xiongnu lost the Ordos and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu-corridor">Gansu corridor</a>; when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-xiongnu">Northern Xiongnu</a> suffered famine and plagues of locusts in the 80s CE, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> defeated them and absorbed most of their people. Nomad dynasties that settled in conquered lands were absorbed or driven out, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khaldun">Ibn Khaldun</a>’s cycle predicted: the Mongol Yuan were expelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 1368, less than a century after Kublai proclaimed the dynasty.</p><p>Conquest by steppe armies could be devastating. The Mongol campaigns against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazmian-empire">Khwarazmian Empire</a> in 1219–1221 destroyed cities such as Urgench, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merv">Merv</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nishapur">Nishapur</a>, and in 1258 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hulagu-khan">Hulagu Khan</a>’s army sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and put the last Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, al-Musta’sim, to death. Persian historians of the period, such as Ata-Malik Juvayni, recorded massacres on a vast scale, and although their figures cannot be trusted, the losses of people and irrigation works in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> were long felt. In the end the confederations were themselves overwhelmed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> empires: Peter Perdue has argued that the treaties of Nerchinsk (1689) and Kyakhta (1727) between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing China</a> closed the steppe and left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungars</a> nowhere to turn.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Impact &amp; Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/genghis-khan.jpg" alt="Genghis Khan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:YuanEmperorAlbumGenghisPortrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Steppe empires left their titles and institutions across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurasia">Eurasia</a>. Khan became a royal title from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>, and Turkic and Mongol rulers long held that only descendants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> could bear it: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>, who was not one, ruled through puppet khans and called himself güregen, “son-in-law”, after marrying into the line, and the Mughal emperors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> were proud to descend from both Timur and Genghis Khan. Historians such as Donald Ostrowski have argued that Muscovy borrowed much of its administration from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>: the postal relay, the yam, survived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and the Russian word for customs, tamozhnya, comes from the tamga, a Mongol levy on trade. The names of modern assemblies, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-great-khural">State Great Khural</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurultai">kurultais</a> of Kazakhstan, Kyrgyzstan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bashkortostan">Bashkortostan</a>, recall the old assembly, and Mongolia’s Naadam festival keeps the wrestling, horse racing and archery that accompanied it.</p><p>Judgements of the steppe empires have changed greatly. Chinese, Persian, Russian and European chroniclers, who wrote most of the surviving accounts, cast the nomads as barbarians and destroyers. Twentieth-century scholars such as René Grousset in L’Empire des steppes (1939) and Owen Lattimore in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-asia">Inner Asian</a> Frontiers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> (1940) treated them instead as part of a single Eurasian history, in which steppe and sown depended on each other. Barfield’s The Perilous Frontier (1989) traced a cycle in which nomadic empires rose and fell with the Chinese dynasties they lived off, from 221 BCE to the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungars</a> in 1757.</p><p>Debate continues over what these polities were. Kradin and others class them as chiefdoms of exceptional complexity, Di Cosmo as states forged in war, and Sneath, in The Headless State (2007), argues that aristocratic power rather than tribal kinship held them together. Modern Mongolia and Kazakhstan claim the steppe empires as national heritage: in 2006 Mongolia marked the 800th anniversary of the kurultai of 1206 that founded the Great Mongol State.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The nomadic confederation was the steppe’s answer to the farming empires on its edges: herding tribes joined under one ruling clan and khan, organised as a single army and sustained by plunder, tribute and trade. From the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> of 209 BCE to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungars</a> destroyed in 1757 it produced the largest land empire in history and shaped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</em></p>

